00:02:38.534 --> 00:02:42.634
she did the puzzle in pen,
even on the hard days.

00:02:42.634 --> 00:02:48.770
She didn't like anyone kind of
touching her New York Times.

00:02:48.770 --> 00:02:51.939
Like, she wanted a pristine puzzle.

00:02:51.939 --> 00:02:54.458
She don't like to tear the puzzle out.

00:02:54.458 --> 00:02:57.567
So, we had two home
delivery subscriptions.

00:02:57.567 --> 00:03:04.305
And I can remember, at a very young age,
when I was probably 12 getting real,

00:03:04.305 --> 00:03:09.656
I got very interested in movies,
the old movies at that point.

00:03:09.656 --> 00:03:11.233
And just becoming so

00:03:11.233 --> 00:03:16.908
devoted to like the arts pages of
The Times were sort of my introduction.

00:03:16.908 --> 00:03:22.398
If I wanted to know, culturally,
what was happening in New York,

00:03:22.398 --> 00:03:27.347
I'd grab for the arts page of-
&gt;&gt; Well, you went from an avid

00:03:27.347 --> 00:03:31.820
reader to launching your
career in this industry.

00:03:31.820 --> 00:03:34.150
You started with the Legal Times, and

00:03:34.150 --> 00:03:37.940
spent almost a decade at the Wall Street
Journal, as an investigative journalist.

00:03:37.940 --> 00:03:38.610
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:03:38.610 --> 00:03:41.230
So, most of us here probably don't

00:03:41.230 --> 00:03:45.245
quite have an idea of what exactly is
