00:03:45.245 --> 00:03:47.760
Can you tell us a little
bit what that was like and

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:50.010
some of the early challenges
you had to overcome?

00:03:51.810 --> 00:03:57.268
&gt;&gt; Well, but the life of an investigative
reporter is often the key if you

00:03:57.268 --> 00:04:03.297
want to get hold of secret, confidential
documents to buttress your story.

00:04:03.297 --> 00:04:08.981
Sometimes it's lonely work,
kind of green, old green eyeshade,

00:04:08.981 --> 00:04:16.061
kind of work of just sifting through piles
of documents that you may have been given,

00:04:16.061 --> 00:04:21.164
looking for the diamonds in the rough,
in those documents.

00:04:21.164 --> 00:04:28.072
It can be trying to locate
people in remote places.

00:04:28.072 --> 00:04:33.590
I mean, a vivid memory of mine from
back at the Wall Street Journal.

00:04:33.590 --> 00:04:38.071
It's not one of my daughter's
favorite memories, but

00:04:38.071 --> 00:04:43.889
I was doing some Investigative reporting
on overseas donations that had

00:04:43.889 --> 00:04:51.350
come to the Democratic National Committee
during Bill Clinton's reelection in 1996.

00:04:51.350 --> 00:04:55.959
And these donations were being
given through straw donors,

00:04:55.959 --> 00:05:01.755
the money hadn't really been the
individuals who reported the donations.

00:05:01.755 --> 00:05:05.169
But their addresses were listed and

00:05:05.169 --> 00:05:10.403
many of these people lived
in communities surrounding

00:05:10.403 --> 00:05:14.739
in suburban Maryland, suburban Virginia.

00:05:14.739 --> 00:05:18.954
And when I began driving to
try to go to these addresses,

00:05:18.954 --> 00:05:23.800
I could see immediately that it
was very unlikely that these were

00:05:23.800 --> 00:05:28.304
people who could afford to
give $50,000 to the DNC.

00:05:28.304 --> 00:05:35.963
And my daughter at that point was playing
on a rotating soccer team in that area.

00:05:35.963 --> 00:05:40.116
So I'd go to her soccer game and
then make her get in the car and

00:05:40.116 --> 00:05:45.003
spend a couple of hours with investigative
reporter mom like going up to

00:05:45.003 --> 00:05:47.700
these houses and knocking on the door.

00:05:47.700 --> 00:05:52.730
And I remember her saying to me,
like, mom you can't do that.

00:05:52.730 --> 00:05:54.850
[LAUGH] You know, it's not polite.

00:05:54.850 --> 00:05:58.140
But, it's a little bit of
that kind of work too.

00:05:58.140 --> 00:06:01.220
It's like old fashioned gumshoe reporting.

00:06:02.700 --> 00:06:04.343
That isn't the first reporting I did.

00:06:04.343 --> 00:06:09.717
The first job I got out of Harvard,
I had been a stringer for

00:06:09.717 --> 00:06:16.192
Time Magazine while I was at Harvard,
covering different stories and

00:06:16.192 --> 00:06:21.812
notable professors and
other things on the Harvard campus.

00:06:21.812 --> 00:06:27.975
For you business students, that my most
vivid memory of that early reporting was,

00:06:27.975 --> 00:06:33.609
I was constantly calling up John Kenneth
Galbraith, and asking him over and

00:06:33.609 --> 00:06:38.118
over again, is the West in decline,
Professor Galbraith?

00:06:38.118 --> 00:06:42.430
It got to be kind of a joke between us.

00:06:42.430 --> 00:06:46.510
Like, if it was Friday it was like,
hi, Professor Galbraith, it's Jill.

00:06:46.510 --> 00:06:48.440
Is the west in decline this week?

00:06:50.510 --> 00:06:53.560
It was part-time reporting for
Time magazine.

00:06:55.180 --> 00:06:59.905
At that point, the Boston bureau
chief of Time was a woman

00:06:59.905 --> 00:07:04.838
named Sandra Burton,
who had begun at Time as a secretary.

00:07:04.838 --> 00:07:10.076
And she had literally,
through dint of being a great curious

00:07:10.076 --> 00:07:14.905
reporter and journalist,
literally clawed her way out

00:07:14.905 --> 00:07:19.852
of the secretarial pool into
the correspondents ranks.

00:07:19.852 --> 00:07:25.420
And she had become the first first
female bureau chief at Time in Boston.

00:07:25.420 --> 00:07:32.186
And she was my first boss, and
she was incredibly encouraging to me.

00:07:32.186 --> 00:07:37.402
And it was meaningful to her that even
though I was a freshly minted Harvard

00:07:37.402 --> 00:07:42.535
student, I got a permanent job in
their office right after graduation,

00:07:42.535 --> 00:07:47.521
that I get some meaningful experience and
learn how to really report.

00:07:47.521 --> 00:07:52.385
And, do some hard reporting too, and

00:07:52.385 --> 00:07:59.990
She then went on to be based in Asia for
a time, in Hong Kong.

00:07:59.990 --> 00:08:03.000
And when I became Washington Bureau Chief

00:08:04.930 --> 00:08:09.690
of the Times in 2000,
I was the first woman to have that job.

00:08:09.690 --> 00:08:11.130
She sent me a telegram,

00:08:11.130 --> 00:08:17.310
it was long enough ago that telegrams
were still being sent and her telegram

00:08:17.310 --> 00:08:22.940
to me said she could hear the glass
shattering all the way to Hong Kong.

00:08:22.940 --> 00:08:30.670
She was my first boss, and the only female
boss I ever had for the rest of my career,

00:08:30.670 --> 00:08:35.870
except for the period at the time
when Janet Robinson was CEO

00:08:35.870 --> 00:08:41.000
of the Times and I was managing editor,
I had a lot of interaction with her.

00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:47.070
&gt;&gt; I would love to talk to you
more about the gender dynamics and

00:08:47.070 --> 00:08:50.840
working as a female in
the journalism industry.

00:08:50.840 --> 00:08:56.544
But you mentioned your times at the Times
now, and when now you're in a position

00:08:56.544 --> 00:09:01.753
where you have to decide on a daily
basis what's fit or unfit to publish.

00:09:01.753 --> 00:09:05.932
On one hand, you have obligation to
the audience to tell them the truth,

00:09:05.932 --> 00:09:10.728
and on the other hand I know there's been
instances where the government told you,

00:09:10.728 --> 00:09:14.772
you'll have blood on your hand if
you publish this article relating to

00:09:14.772 --> 00:09:16.900
national security.

00:09:16.900 --> 00:09:19.170
You had to make a lot of tough calls.

00:09:19.170 --> 00:09:24.462
How did you make these calls, and what's
the framework you've used to do that?

00:09:24.462 --> 00:09:29.193
&gt;&gt; It's a great question, and
one interestingly enough that

00:09:29.193 --> 00:09:34.114
we spent three hours in
Professor Phillip Taubman's class,

00:09:34.114 --> 00:09:40.200
where I visited yesterday,
his journalism class discussing just that.

00:09:40.200 --> 00:09:43.843
Because when I was managing
editor of the Times and

00:09:43.843 --> 00:09:46.706
Phil was the Washington Bureau Chief,

00:09:46.706 --> 00:09:51.753
we had to actually consult on a number
of those difficult decisions.

00:09:51.753 --> 00:09:56.927
And it's not like there's
an editing manual that

00:09:56.927 --> 00:10:03.836
teaches you how to come to a perfect
decision in those situations.

00:10:03.836 --> 00:10:10.596
And all of them are somewhat different,
because when the government asks for

00:10:10.596 --> 00:10:14.444
either an entire story
to be not published,

00:10:14.444 --> 00:10:18.916
or what is more common is
the government will ask for

00:10:18.916 --> 00:10:23.492
certain details in a story
to be withheld on national

00:10:23.492 --> 00:10:27.756
security grounds because
they think disclosure

00:10:27.756 --> 00:10:32.670
of certain classified details
may put lives in danger.

00:10:32.670 --> 00:10:37.850
It's agonizing every time you
get one of those requests.

00:10:37.850 --> 00:10:40.485
I think what an editor has to do is,

00:10:40.485 --> 00:10:46.580
I don't wanna sort of
get all heavy on you and

00:10:46.580 --> 00:10:51.310
start invoking the Founders, but
what you have to do first and

00:10:51.310 --> 00:10:56.440
foremost is remind yourself that
the Founders did give the press

00:10:57.900 --> 00:11:01.180
enshrined in the First Amendment,
and it's first for a reason,

00:11:02.270 --> 00:11:07.290
these very special protections,
because they believed so

00:11:07.290 --> 00:11:11.740
vehemently in the importance of our
role in holding power accountable and

00:11:11.740 --> 00:11:16.405
making sure power in this country
didn't become abusive and

00:11:16.405 --> 00:11:19.590
too centralized.

00:11:19.590 --> 00:11:22.330
Because that's what the Founders were so

00:11:22.330 --> 00:11:26.530
deathly afraid of and felt they had
witnessed happening in Britain.

00:11:26.530 --> 00:11:29.570
So you have to kind of
take a deep breath and

00:11:29.570 --> 00:11:34.610
remind yourself that we're here first and
foremost to inform the citizenry.

00:11:34.610 --> 00:11:37.770
To keep the public informed.

00:11:37.770 --> 00:11:43.890
And that to keep genuinely
important newsworthy information

00:11:43.890 --> 00:11:50.740
away from the public goes against
our wiring as journalists,

00:11:50.740 --> 00:11:55.160
but nor
do we want to harm national security.

00:11:55.160 --> 00:11:58.312
And particularly after 9/11,

00:11:58.312 --> 00:12:02.835
the number of requests to withhold
information from stories and

00:12:02.835 --> 00:12:10.795
in a few cases, hold stories back
from publication, began increasing.

00:12:10.795 --> 00:12:14.580
&gt;&gt; Can you give us an example
of how you weigh the tradeoffs?

00:12:14.580 --> 00:12:19.754
&gt;&gt; Sure, I mean one that is
a pretty prominent case that in

00:12:19.754 --> 00:12:24.928
fact has been written about
involved probably the most

00:12:24.928 --> 00:12:29.990
sensitive story about
classified intelligence that

00:12:29.990 --> 00:12:35.295
the Times published when I
was managing it in 2005.

00:12:35.295 --> 00:12:41.176
It was the first story that disclosed
that the NSA was doing warrantless

00:12:41.176 --> 00:12:46.665
eavesdropping, which was actually
illegal because at that point

00:12:46.665 --> 00:12:51.859
the NSA had to go to the FISA court
in each of the cases it wanted to

00:12:51.859 --> 00:12:56.878
do eavesdropping, and
they were bypassing the FISA court.

00:12:56.878 --> 00:13:01.961
They were just downloading
telephone communications.

00:13:01.961 --> 00:13:07.899
And in that case,
the Bush administration pretty immediately

00:13:07.899 --> 00:13:13.165
after the two reporters,
Jim Risen and Eric Lishblam,

00:13:13.165 --> 00:13:18.670
began reporting on it said you
cannot publish that story.

00:13:18.670 --> 00:13:25.795
That program, that eavesdropping Is the
crown jewel in our anti-terrorism program.

00:13:25.795 --> 00:13:32.270
It has Michael Hayden,
a very senior intelligence official,

00:13:32.270 --> 00:13:38.257
made assurances that the NSA
eavesdropping had literally

00:13:38.257 --> 00:13:44.378
helped unplug some terrorism
plots that were in the works.

00:13:44.378 --> 00:13:49.203
And in the Oval Office,
the then executive editor of the Times and

00:13:49.203 --> 00:13:54.115
the publisher of The Times were told
by President Bush if we publish

00:13:54.115 --> 00:13:57.298
the story we would have
blood on our hands.

00:13:57.298 --> 00:14:01.209
And we held it for more than a year, but

00:14:01.209 --> 00:14:06.345
the legality of this kind
of eavesdropping became

00:14:06.345 --> 00:14:12.962
ever clearer as the reporters
continued to amass information.

00:14:12.962 --> 00:14:17.211
And it seemed,
everything is a balancing test.

00:14:17.211 --> 00:14:21.761
It's that, is the information so
compelling and important for

00:14:21.761 --> 00:14:23.200
Americans to know?

00:14:23.200 --> 00:14:27.940
Does that outweigh the possible
risk to national security?

00:14:27.940 --> 00:14:33.290
And in the case of that story
we felt that the public in

00:14:33.290 --> 00:14:37.930
whose name the war on terror was
being waged, they needed to know.

00:14:37.930 --> 00:14:43.260
We weren't sure the public
would be comfortable

00:14:43.260 --> 00:14:46.080
with those kinds of programs, which of
course kind of lead us now into the area

00:14:46.080 --> 00:14:51.125
that Edward Snowden has
made his disclosures about.

00:14:51.125 --> 00:14:56.925
But these are difficult cases, and
I think in all of them and that

00:14:56.925 --> 00:15:02.565
this would be true at the Washington Post
or any other quality news organization,

00:15:02.565 --> 00:15:07.445
that when you get requests from
the government, you hear it out.

00:15:07.445 --> 00:15:12.846
You don't just get a leak and
then slap it into the newspaper without

00:15:12.846 --> 00:15:18.461
hearing out those arguments and
giving them careful consideration.

00:15:18.461 --> 00:15:20.211
&gt;&gt; You have a really tough job.

00:15:20.211 --> 00:15:22.338
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And

00:15:22.338 --> 00:15:26.689
with that I want to talk to you about
your leadership philosophy because,

00:15:26.689 --> 00:15:30.840
as we know, the Times is
an extremely complex organization.

00:15:30.840 --> 00:15:33.030
You have many different stakeholders.

00:15:33.030 --> 00:15:36.480
You have the publisher,
the journalists, the audience, and

00:15:36.480 --> 00:15:39.200
the government sometimes,
as we talked about.

00:15:39.200 --> 00:15:44.190
And unlike most other companies where
they would have a longer product cycle,

00:15:44.190 --> 00:15:47.310
the Times put out a new
product every single day.

00:15:47.310 --> 00:15:48.450
And it can't be buggy.

00:15:48.450 --> 00:15:51.200
&gt;&gt; And now with the web-
&gt;&gt; It's constant.

00:15:51.200 --> 00:15:52.802
&gt;&gt; It's constant, yeah.

00:15:52.802 --> 00:15:56.626
So how do you manage such
a Complex organization, with so

00:15:56.626 --> 00:16:00.220
many moving parts under so
many constant deadlines?

00:16:01.650 --> 00:16:05.590
It's a difficult organization to manage.

00:16:05.590 --> 00:16:06.950
There's no question.

00:16:06.950 --> 00:16:12.395
It's also a thrilling
organization to manage because

00:16:12.395 --> 00:16:18.547
they have the best journalists in
the world who are expert in so

00:16:18.547 --> 00:16:23.536
many subjects,
doing the highest quality work.

00:16:23.536 --> 00:16:30.051
So on any given day,
I'd be reviewing a piece of multimedia

00:16:30.051 --> 00:16:36.453
on the restored Islamic wing
at the Metropolitan Museum.

00:16:36.453 --> 00:16:41.893
And then doing a close reading
of a story involving corruption

00:16:41.893 --> 00:16:47.661
of Chinese princelings and China,
a long investigative story.

00:16:47.661 --> 00:16:54.470
It was an incredibly varied menu
of journalism that I dealt with.

00:16:56.200 --> 00:16:59.440
And journalism I think over time,
it's almost,

00:17:00.690 --> 00:17:04.950
I think, so unlikely that I would have

00:17:04.950 --> 00:17:09.690
become executive editor because my
background was in investigative reporting.

00:17:09.690 --> 00:17:14.055
And typically investigative
reporters are lone wolves.

00:17:14.055 --> 00:17:19.732
You're used to sort of hunting and
going after stories by yourself.

00:17:19.732 --> 00:17:24.700
And journalism now, especially in
a multimedia world, has become so

00:17:24.700 --> 00:17:26.870
much more collaborative.

00:17:26.870 --> 00:17:31.880
The lone wolf story is the exception now.

00:17:31.880 --> 00:17:35.370
I mean,
now most big stories at the Times and

00:17:35.370 --> 00:17:40.140
other places involve teams of journalists,
data experts,

00:17:40.140 --> 00:17:45.820
graphics editors, obviously,
photographers, videographers.

00:17:49.150 --> 00:17:54.980
It's a team sport, and for someone whose
background was investigative journalism,

00:17:54.980 --> 00:18:01.320
I'm not sure I would have been
the natural manager of these large team.

00:18:01.320 --> 00:18:03.120
&gt;&gt; But you did lead as a manager.

00:18:03.120 --> 00:18:05.370
&gt;&gt; Yeah, sure.

00:18:05.370 --> 00:18:13.444
I think that probably my best leadership
quality was just my passion for

00:18:13.444 --> 00:18:20.860
going after ambitious stories
that were gonna have high impact.

00:18:22.060 --> 00:18:27.804
And that I was talented at being
able to think up ideas for

00:18:27.804 --> 00:18:34.661
my colleagues that would be those
kinds of high-impact stories.

00:18:34.661 --> 00:18:41.148
But the creative side of
storymaking was my background,

00:18:41.148 --> 00:18:46.824
and obviously,
as I rose up in the organization,

00:18:46.824 --> 00:18:52.227
I had to learn on the fly
all of the things that you

00:18:52.227 --> 00:18:57.770
are studying here at
Stanford Business School,

00:18:57.770 --> 00:19:00.900
which is business models.

00:19:02.850 --> 00:19:05.583
Journalism is under stress,

00:19:05.583 --> 00:19:11.280
i turns out that's the least
well-kept secret in the world.

00:19:11.280 --> 00:19:12.161
&gt;&gt; Well, we will get to that.

00:19:12.161 --> 00:19:16.009
&gt;&gt; The whole time that I
was managing editor and

00:19:16.009 --> 00:19:21.940
then executive editor,
it was the business model is under stress.

00:19:21.940 --> 00:19:27.070
Also, it's a thrilling time because
we're making the big digital

00:19:27.070 --> 00:19:31.560
transition from the print past
into the digital present.

00:19:32.730 --> 00:19:34.430
And that presented its own challenges.

00:19:36.170 --> 00:19:39.710
&gt;&gt; You clearly had to
learn these skills as

00:19:39.710 --> 00:19:44.580
your role changed from being an individual
reporter to now running the newsroom.

00:19:44.580 --> 00:19:48.411
&gt;&gt; Right, I wish maybe that the Times
had sent me here for a year.

00:19:48.411 --> 00:19:51.056
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:19:52.790 --> 00:19:54.260
&gt;&gt; Derek Bolton should take note of that.

00:19:54.260 --> 00:20:00.536
So, talking more about your
management philosophy.

00:20:00.536 --> 00:20:05.801
I want to talk about your
management style at the Times, and

00:20:05.801 --> 00:20:11.802
I read in other media outlets as
well that you were described as, and

00:20:11.802 --> 00:20:17.300
I quote, abrasive, too pushy,
polarizing, impossible.

00:20:18.610 --> 00:20:22.010
Did these come as shock to
you when you first read them?

00:20:23.730 --> 00:20:28.830
&gt;&gt; You know,
I was obviously not happy to read those.

00:20:28.830 --> 00:20:32.530
I wouldn't say I was completely surprised.

00:20:32.530 --> 00:20:39.106
I was aware that my
style is hard-charging,

00:20:39.106 --> 00:20:44.642
I hope you can tell, fairly direct and

00:20:44.642 --> 00:20:48.286
direct in the way I talk.

00:20:48.286 --> 00:20:54.288
I guess that, through the eyes of
some people could be seen as brusque.

00:20:54.288 --> 00:20:59.364
I was always very ambitious for
the Times to have the best story,

00:20:59.364 --> 00:21:06.920
and whether that sometimes translated into
pushiness, that wouldn't surprise me.

00:21:06.920 --> 00:21:13.700
If that's so,
I felt the bottom line is that for

00:21:13.700 --> 00:21:20.280
many journalists that I had worked with,
I inspired their best work.

00:21:20.280 --> 00:21:25.328
And that that was very important and
my major strength.

00:21:25.328 --> 00:21:31.600
I think that so many of the words
that you have just read are so

00:21:31.600 --> 00:21:37.373
personality-focused, and
I'm certainly aware and

00:21:37.373 --> 00:21:42.140
have read quite a lot of
literature on the fact

00:21:42.140 --> 00:21:46.531
that when women are in leadership roles,

00:21:46.531 --> 00:21:54.311
often it is those stylistic personal
issues that just get outsize focus,

00:21:54.311 --> 00:21:59.860
and are kind of picked over to death, but-
&gt;&gt; Well,

00:22:01.990 --> 00:22:05.490
do you think, on that note,
women are scrutinized in an unfair way?

00:22:06.660 --> 00:22:11.996
&gt;&gt; I think sometime they're scrutinized
in a way that is a double standard.

00:22:11.996 --> 00:22:17.528
That qualities that are seen
in a negative light,

00:22:17.528 --> 00:22:21.084
when applied to women leaders,

00:22:21.084 --> 00:22:26.495
are sometimes seen as
successful traits in men.

00:22:28.953 --> 00:22:32.620
&gt;&gt; As we-
&gt;&gt; I want to also just kind of pause

00:22:32.620 --> 00:22:37.100
here to say,
I don't anyone to misunderstand and

00:22:37.100 --> 00:22:41.550
think I'm trying to say that I
was fired because I'm a woman.

00:22:41.550 --> 00:22:46.300
I totally don't think that.

00:22:46.300 --> 00:22:53.870
I think that some of the perceptions of
me were colored by some of these issues.

00:22:53.870 --> 00:22:59.508
But I think that there were many issues

00:22:59.508 --> 00:23:06.555
that came into play in
terms of the denouement,

00:23:06.555 --> 00:23:10.605
and me getting fired, so,

00:23:10.605 --> 00:23:15.560
just wanted to say that plainly.

00:23:15.560 --> 00:23:20.120
&gt;&gt; What, if anything, do you think
you could have done differently?

00:23:20.120 --> 00:23:24.830
&gt;&gt; I think that,
I've thought lots about that and

00:23:24.830 --> 00:23:29.558
things that if I could do over,
I would do over.

00:23:29.558 --> 00:23:35.468
I definitely would have spent
more time listening and

00:23:35.468 --> 00:23:38.495
less time talking myself.

00:23:38.495 --> 00:23:45.178
I think because of the period of time
I matured in my career that I had so

00:23:45.178 --> 00:23:51.631
often been in the situation in
a meeting where I was the only woman,

00:23:51.631 --> 00:23:56.816
where I felt I wasn't heard or
listened to that I think

00:23:56.816 --> 00:24:01.888
perhaps by the time I got
into the big leadership jobs

00:24:01.888 --> 00:24:07.191
at the Times I was overcompensating
by making sure I was

00:24:07.191 --> 00:24:12.300
heard in a way that I think
probably didn't help me.

00:24:12.300 --> 00:24:13.946
So listening more.

00:24:13.946 --> 00:24:21.670
I think that because this was a period
of economic stress in the newsroom,

00:24:21.670 --> 00:24:26.156
and I was so
determined to fight to preserve

00:24:26.156 --> 00:24:31.016
every job of every
journalist in that newsroom

00:24:31.016 --> 00:24:35.751
that I probably was not
the easiest person for

00:24:35.751 --> 00:24:42.255
some of my business side colleagues
to deal with all the time.

00:24:42.255 --> 00:24:50.125
I was very protective of the newsroom of
the New York Times and it's prerogative.

00:24:50.125 --> 00:24:54.045
There had, for
my whole career, been a very

00:24:57.390 --> 00:25:04.800
clear Chinese wall, it was always called
then, between the news and the business.

00:25:04.800 --> 00:25:12.354
And I had sort of been trained to be
very protective of the newsroom and

00:25:12.354 --> 00:25:19.140
to somewhat keep the business
side at bay on certain matters.

00:25:20.150 --> 00:25:24.980
And I think that in a digital world, and

00:25:24.980 --> 00:25:30.220
now I see the Times has
more jobs that are joint

00:25:30.220 --> 00:25:33.350
reports to the business side and
the news side.

00:25:33.350 --> 00:25:37.858
&gt;From my period of time I was
very anxious about that and

00:25:37.858 --> 00:25:42.468
probably pretty resistant to
it because I thought that

00:25:42.468 --> 00:25:47.793
the journalism was best served
by being at arms distance from.

00:25:49.561 --> 00:25:54.387
The advertising and
circulation sides of the paper.

00:25:54.387 --> 00:25:59.635
And I think maybe looking back on it,
I was too protective.

00:25:59.635 --> 00:26:04.389
&gt;&gt; And as we all here today walk out of
the auditorium to strive to change lives,

00:26:04.389 --> 00:26:09.069
organization in the world, what are the
things that we can do as both men and

00:26:09.069 --> 00:26:12.150
women to change the situation
at our workplace?

00:26:14.540 --> 00:26:19.420
&gt;&gt; Well, I think you have to be clear and

00:26:19.420 --> 00:26:24.160
focused in what you want to change,
first and foremost.

00:26:24.160 --> 00:26:28.704
I don't think it's effective always
to have ten things that you wanna

00:26:28.704 --> 00:26:30.486
change in your workplace.

00:26:30.486 --> 00:26:37.488
But if there's one transcendent issue that
you really think needs to be transformed

00:26:37.488 --> 00:26:43.518
and if it was that it would make where
you're working so much healthier.

00:26:43.518 --> 00:26:50.570
I think being direct and not necessarily

00:26:50.570 --> 00:26:55.650
being overly political, or
stepping on people's toes and

00:26:55.650 --> 00:27:00.130
bringing that issue to the attention
of the people who run the company.

00:27:00.130 --> 00:27:04.319
I think you should be
pretty forthright and

00:27:05.540 --> 00:27:09.250
kind of kick ass about
trying to make change.

00:27:09.250 --> 00:27:10.150
I really do.

00:27:11.420 --> 00:27:15.940
&gt;&gt; You've definitely made a lot
of change in your life and

00:27:15.940 --> 00:27:18.210
at the organization that you've been at.

00:27:18.210 --> 00:27:22.040
And even after you got
fired from the Times,

00:27:22.040 --> 00:27:26.480
Dean Saloner mentioned that you
were giving a commencement speech.

00:27:26.480 --> 00:27:28.390
&gt;&gt; Yeah,
it wasn't two months after though.

00:27:28.390 --> 00:27:32.450
&gt;&gt; It was almost like-
&gt;&gt; It was just a few days after.

00:27:32.450 --> 00:27:36.430
It like the Sunday, I was fired on
a Wednesday, I think it was the following

00:27:36.430 --> 00:27:41.600
Sunday I had agreed to be
the commencement speaker at Wake Forest.

00:27:41.600 --> 00:27:46.484
And they were worried that
I was gonna withdraw, but

00:27:46.484 --> 00:27:49.500
I had said yes and I was gonna go.

00:27:49.500 --> 00:27:53.040
&gt;&gt; And I love this quote you said,
which is I'm talking to

00:27:53.040 --> 00:27:57.340
anyone who has been dumped, have not
gotten the job you really wanted, or

00:27:57.340 --> 00:27:59.930
have received those
horrible rejection letters.

00:27:59.930 --> 00:28:02.140
You know the disappointment of losing.

00:28:02.140 --> 00:28:04.560
When that happens,
show what you are made of.

00:28:05.650 --> 00:28:06.925
So what are you made of, Jill?

00:28:06.925 --> 00:28:11.790
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:28:11.790 --> 00:28:16.007
&gt;&gt; Well, I think I'm made up of,

00:28:16.007 --> 00:28:20.725
I am a resilient person, I think.

00:28:20.725 --> 00:28:25.535
And I think anyone, who, when a lot of
you, I hate to tell you even you wonderful

00:28:27.585 --> 00:28:31.315
Stanford business grads,
some of you are gonna get fired in

00:28:32.735 --> 00:28:33.941
the course of your-

00:28:33.941 --> 00:28:34.977
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Career.

00:28:34.977 --> 00:28:38.120
And work on that resilience thing.

00:28:38.120 --> 00:28:41.546
It really helps.

00:28:45.426 --> 00:28:51.265
I think I'm, I love to read,
I get a tremendous,

00:28:51.265 --> 00:28:55.991
I just think the Times is still the best,

00:28:55.991 --> 00:29:02.145
it's the most enjoyable
thing to sit down and read.

00:29:02.145 --> 00:29:07.440
And I'm made up of someone
who just has tremendous

00:29:07.440 --> 00:29:12.130
appreciation for
a beautifully crafted story.

00:29:12.130 --> 00:29:14.920
I'm a very involved family person.

00:29:14.920 --> 00:29:17.320
I have two children.

00:29:17.320 --> 00:29:20.910
I met my husband my
sophomore year at Harvard.

00:29:20.910 --> 00:29:23.420
We've been together since we're 20.

00:29:23.420 --> 00:29:26.219
I'm a big dog person.

00:29:26.219 --> 00:29:28.149
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; In fact you wrote

00:29:28.149 --> 00:29:29.250
a book about your dog.

00:29:29.250 --> 00:29:31.849
&gt;&gt; I did,
I wrote a whole book about my dog.

00:29:31.849 --> 00:29:35.870
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Silly though that may seem.

00:29:38.005 --> 00:29:42.703
But I think that what I'm made of,

00:29:42.703 --> 00:29:46.438
it goes back to my parents.

00:29:46.438 --> 00:29:53.240
And my mother was very influential
in making me love reading so much.

00:29:53.240 --> 00:29:58.388
But I had a father who really thought
he had two daughters that if we

00:29:58.388 --> 00:30:04.130
set our mind to something,
we could do it if we worked hard enough.

00:30:04.130 --> 00:30:09.322
And he even, when Daylight Savings Time
would come on the East Coast,

00:30:09.322 --> 00:30:13.898
he would take a bat and a ball,
I was never a very good athlete,

00:30:13.898 --> 00:30:16.979
out to Central Park and he'd have me hit.

00:30:16.979 --> 00:30:21.605
And I'd be like I can't do it and
he'd be sure you can Jilly, sure you can.

00:30:21.605 --> 00:30:24.669
He was very encouraging.

00:30:24.669 --> 00:30:30.377
And the morning that it became public

00:30:30.377 --> 00:30:37.430
that I'd been fired my
sister called me up.

00:30:37.430 --> 00:30:38.970
And she said to me, you know what?

00:30:38.970 --> 00:30:40.978
My father passed away in 1992.

00:30:40.978 --> 00:30:44.898
But she said, our dad would be
as proud of you today as he was

00:30:44.898 --> 00:30:48.670
when you became
Executive Editor of the Times.

00:30:48.670 --> 00:30:53.040
And that was very meaningful to me.

00:30:53.040 --> 00:30:58.860
And it made me all the more
wanna go to Wake Forest and

00:30:58.860 --> 00:31:01.250
give that speech.

00:31:01.250 --> 00:31:05.020
And that morning I had an appointment.

00:31:05.020 --> 00:31:06.190
I'm also a gym rat.

00:31:06.190 --> 00:31:08.200
That's what I'm made of, and that's right.

00:31:08.200 --> 00:31:09.620
&gt;&gt; I saw that on Instagram.

00:31:09.620 --> 00:31:11.720
&gt;&gt; So that's what I was gonna say.

00:31:11.720 --> 00:31:14.910
The next thing I did, I had
an appointment to do personal training.

00:31:14.910 --> 00:31:20.120
So I went to see my trainer, and when I
got there, he had these big red boxing

00:31:20.120 --> 00:31:25.220
gloves and he handed them to me and
he said you need those today.

00:31:26.450 --> 00:31:31.516
And he had on his big red boxing gloves,
and for the next hour,

00:31:31.516 --> 00:31:35.529
I just like whacked at his
big red boxing gloves.

00:31:35.529 --> 00:31:38.795
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And it was incredibly therapeutic.

00:31:38.795 --> 00:31:41.671
I hadn't brought my phone, but

00:31:41.671 --> 00:31:46.373
I said to Gene my trainer I said,
do you have a phone?

00:31:46.373 --> 00:31:49.588
Cuz I want to send my kids a picture.

00:31:49.588 --> 00:31:56.260
I was worried that my kids would think
I was crying in bed or something.

00:31:56.260 --> 00:32:00.588
So I wanted to show them mom
in the cool red boxing gloves.

00:32:00.588 --> 00:32:01.672
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And so

00:32:01.672 --> 00:32:07.370
he sent me a picture from his phone,
I sent it to them.

00:32:07.370 --> 00:32:11.493
Then I went home, and
about 11:00 in the morning,

00:32:11.493 --> 00:32:17.474
my daughter who is a surgeon in Boston,
I didn't know, she put it on Instagram.

00:32:17.474 --> 00:32:20.634
&gt;&gt; She did.
&gt;&gt; She called me up and she said Mommy,

00:32:20.634 --> 00:32:23.015
that picture's going viral.

00:32:23.015 --> 00:32:27.050
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And it was the wood,

00:32:27.050 --> 00:32:32.390
which is the big top picture on
the next day's New York Post.

00:32:32.390 --> 00:32:37.557
I must say it wasn't the most
flattering picture of me in like a.

00:32:39.543 --> 00:32:45.330
Shirt but I guess that's me, too.

00:32:46.870 --> 00:32:50.074
The fighting person.

00:32:50.074 --> 00:32:53.881
&gt;&gt; Absolutely, and we also sent
that picture around to the GSVS.

00:32:53.881 --> 00:32:54.916
&gt;&gt; Oh no!

00:32:54.916 --> 00:33:02.890
[LAUGH] That picture in the Quad is so
much more attractive than that.

00:33:02.890 --> 00:33:07.570
&gt;&gt; Before we open up the questions for Q&amp;A
for the audience I wanna take some time

00:33:07.570 --> 00:33:10.620
to get your thoughts on the future
of the journalism industry.

00:33:11.640 --> 00:33:14.680
In five to ten years how do you
see people consuming media?

00:33:16.460 --> 00:33:18.080
&gt;&gt; In every way possible.

00:33:18.080 --> 00:33:24.380
I just was reading a story today that the
editor of USA Today was predicting in five

00:33:24.380 --> 00:33:30.220
or six years that they wouldn't
be a print paper anymore.

00:33:30.220 --> 00:33:35.580
But I certainly think the Times is
going to be around in print for

00:33:35.580 --> 00:33:37.510
quite a while longer.

00:33:37.510 --> 00:33:40.620
There's just a pretty robust,

00:33:40.620 --> 00:33:45.806
I think something like
800,000 home delivery

00:33:45.806 --> 00:33:50.885
print subscribers to
the Times here in California.

00:33:50.885 --> 00:33:55.865
Evidently, that costs you who
like to get it in the blue bag,

00:33:55.865 --> 00:33:58.902
more than $900 a year, I think.

00:33:58.902 --> 00:34:03.422
So but, there's a healthy
number of people still who love

00:34:03.422 --> 00:34:06.872
the serendipity of reading in print.

00:34:06.872 --> 00:34:12.050
But news, like I'm actually,

00:34:12.050 --> 00:34:16.370
I'm writing a book about all
the changes in the news industry.

00:34:16.370 --> 00:34:20.095
And you know Snapchat
just hired away CNN's,

00:34:20.095 --> 00:34:23.257
one of their top political reporters.

00:34:23.257 --> 00:34:27.898
Snapchat is going to be
covering the campaign.

00:34:27.898 --> 00:34:34.350
Facebook is obviously one of
the largest distributors of news.

00:34:34.350 --> 00:34:40.418
So many people, their exposure to news
stories is coming in their Facebook feeds.

00:34:40.418 --> 00:34:44.436
And increasingly,
I think the media business and

00:34:44.436 --> 00:34:48.557
the tech business are kind
of merging in an odd way.

00:34:48.557 --> 00:34:53.309
I don't know that the tech companies
are entirely comfortable stepping up and

00:34:53.309 --> 00:34:57.270
taking some of the responsibilities
like we were talking about,

00:34:57.270 --> 00:35:01.240
like having to think, do you hold
the story or publish the story.

00:35:01.240 --> 00:35:05.270
They're relying on places like
the New York Times or Buzzfeed to make

00:35:06.440 --> 00:35:12.130
those decisions for them, but
I don't know that that will last forever.

00:35:12.130 --> 00:35:15.195
&gt;&gt; There's a recent deal with-
&gt;&gt; Everything Vine is a way,

00:35:15.195 --> 00:35:20.680
these very short videos is
a major way news is spreading.

00:35:20.680 --> 00:35:26.480
I think that broadly, rather than just
rattle off like a zillion changes,

00:35:26.480 --> 00:35:31.340
I think the most interesting change
that's happening is that in my lifetime,

00:35:32.450 --> 00:35:39.100
the power to decide what people
read resided in the editors'

00:35:39.100 --> 00:35:44.760
offices of the major newspapers and
broadcast networks.

00:35:44.760 --> 00:35:47.267
&gt;&gt; It resided in your hands.

00:35:47.267 --> 00:35:51.320
In the beginning of me having those jobs,
it was in my hands.

00:35:51.320 --> 00:35:55.468
I picked,
in consultation with other editors.

00:35:55.468 --> 00:35:59.450
But the six stories that would be on
the front page of the New York Times.

00:35:59.450 --> 00:36:03.944
And that those would automatically
be the most important stories

00:36:03.944 --> 00:36:06.239
that the world would be reading.

00:36:06.239 --> 00:36:11.388
But now we have gone to
a social model where more

00:36:11.388 --> 00:36:17.068
readers are actually looking
at news that is picked

00:36:17.068 --> 00:36:22.086
by their friends,
by people that they know,

00:36:22.086 --> 00:36:27.653
not by Jill Abramson,
the editor of a newspaper.

00:36:27.653 --> 00:36:35.940
And fewer readers are actually going to
a single destination to get their news.

00:36:35.940 --> 00:36:42.380
They're reading links from all over the
place, so these are huge, huge changes.

00:36:42.380 --> 00:36:46.366
And the thing that
interests me most of all,

00:36:46.366 --> 00:36:50.891
because I think we can agree
we're living in an age

00:36:50.891 --> 00:36:55.446
that I might call the age
of too much information.

00:36:55.446 --> 00:36:57.013
There's so much news.

00:36:57.013 --> 00:37:02.476
But I haven't seen a lot of great data
on whether that has made society,

00:37:02.476 --> 00:37:08.030
and particularly younger people I
care about, now that I'm teaching,

00:37:08.030 --> 00:37:14.061
whether it's actually made them more
deeply informed than they were before.

00:37:14.061 --> 00:37:17.890
I'm very interested in
exploring that question.

00:37:17.890 --> 00:37:18.540
&gt;&gt; We'll see that with time.

00:37:20.030 --> 00:37:22.950
And with that,
I want to open up the floor for questions.

00:37:22.950 --> 00:37:24.060
There are two mics on.

00:37:24.060 --> 00:37:27.830
Nino has one over here,
Caroline has one over there.

00:37:27.830 --> 00:37:30.270
Please raise your hand and
make sure they see you.

00:37:30.270 --> 00:37:34.609
We're also taking questions from Twitter,
#gsbvftt.

00:37:34.609 --> 00:37:37.910
Let's start with a Twitter question.

00:37:37.910 --> 00:37:39.880
&gt;&gt; Hi, I have two tweets actually.

00:37:39.880 --> 00:37:43.000
The first is in response to your
desire to spend a year at Stanford.

00:37:43.000 --> 00:37:47.100
The MSX program tweeted that
you're welcome to come anytime.

00:37:47.100 --> 00:37:51.759
So the second is-
&gt;&gt; But I'm not on top anymore,

00:37:51.759 --> 00:37:55.590
what will I do with all of
those management skills?

00:37:56.670 --> 00:37:58.180
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; They've made the offer.

00:37:58.180 --> 00:38:03.500
The second thing is a question
from the crowd about

00:38:03.500 --> 00:38:05.860
you talked about the future of media.

00:38:05.860 --> 00:38:09.320
And there's a question about
whether there's a place for

00:38:09.320 --> 00:38:13.410
smaller regional, more localized
coverage either in print or digital.

00:38:13.410 --> 00:38:16.080
And whether you're seeing any business
models out there that are in that

00:38:16.080 --> 00:38:18.195
space and that are working.

00:38:18.195 --> 00:38:23.391
&gt;&gt; Yeah, the question about local news
is one that really has troubled me.

00:38:23.391 --> 00:38:27.872
Because as regional and local newspapers,
which have been dependent on

00:38:27.872 --> 00:38:32.950
classified advertising as their business
model, they have been just hammered.

00:38:34.370 --> 00:38:39.476
And there are literally state legislatures
and city councils going uncovered now.

00:38:39.476 --> 00:38:44.387
And that is just the press not
living up to the role that

00:38:44.387 --> 00:38:49.312
the founders had, for
us to keep power accountable.

00:38:49.312 --> 00:38:53.600
But I do see rays of hope.

00:38:53.600 --> 00:39:00.241
And as I've been distressed at
the atrophying of local and

00:39:00.241 --> 00:39:05.528
regional publications,
there are some great

00:39:05.528 --> 00:39:12.670
digital news rooms that have
arisen focused on local news.

00:39:12.670 --> 00:39:17.825
I was very supportive when
I worked at the Times,

00:39:17.825 --> 00:39:24.365
we partnered with the Texas Tribune,
which was started six or

00:39:24.365 --> 00:39:28.765
seven years ago by a guy named Evan Smith,

00:39:28.765 --> 00:39:33.796
who was editor of
the Texas Monthly Magazine and

00:39:33.796 --> 00:39:36.731
it covers Texas politics.

00:39:36.731 --> 00:39:43.505
And they have I think a newsroom of 50
reporters focused on that, it's fantastic.

00:39:43.505 --> 00:39:50.403
There's a digital publication called
MinnPost, in Minneapolis, that's fabulous.

00:39:50.403 --> 00:39:54.794
The Texas Tribune's business model,
it's a nonprofit.

00:39:54.794 --> 00:39:59.106
So Evan Smith has to go out and
raise money constantly, but

00:39:59.106 --> 00:40:05.011
it's a successful model, and they do
The highest quality kind of journalism.

00:40:05.011 --> 00:40:07.555
I just wish that could be
replicated in more localities.

00:40:07.555 --> 00:40:13.528
&gt;&gt; They have a question over there.

00:40:13.528 --> 00:40:14.570
&gt;&gt; Is it on?
Yeah.

00:40:14.570 --> 00:40:18.320
So kinda along of the same lines
I was interested to hear that.

00:40:20.280 --> 00:40:24.790
So if your book is about
shorter hits of news yet

00:40:24.790 --> 00:40:26.800
your background is in
investigative journalism.

00:40:26.800 --> 00:40:30.280
And I know you were looking at-
&gt;&gt; Why do you think it's about shorter

00:40:30.280 --> 00:40:31.780
hits of news?

00:40:31.780 --> 00:40:35.028
&gt;&gt; Sort of this is what you said around
six seconds from Vine or SnapChat.

00:40:35.028 --> 00:40:36.195
&gt;&gt; Oh well that.

00:40:36.195 --> 00:40:37.404
Yeah.

00:40:37.404 --> 00:40:38.953
&gt;&gt; Right.
And then that's sort of being

00:40:38.953 --> 00:40:42.133
an increasing way that we consume, but
I know that you were sort of working on

00:40:42.133 --> 00:40:45.111
a venture around investigative journalism
&gt;&gt; Of long form, right.

00:40:45.111 --> 00:40:47.980
&gt;&gt; And one big story.

00:40:47.980 --> 00:40:50.509
So I wonder how you
sort of square the two,

00:40:50.509 --> 00:40:55.363
especially sort of thinking about the
younger generation and how that fits in.

00:40:55.363 --> 00:40:58.390
&gt;&gt; Right.
I think if you have a whale of a story

00:40:58.390 --> 00:41:02.090
that's long, it finds a big audience.

00:41:02.090 --> 00:41:06.670
I mean at the Times we found some
of the most challenging, long,

00:41:06.670 --> 00:41:11.700
investigative stories zoomed to
most shared and most emailed.

00:41:11.700 --> 00:41:17.030
So I don't believe everything
has to be short and

00:41:17.030 --> 00:41:22.420
catered to the short attention span or
the size of a smart phone screen.

00:41:25.050 --> 00:41:32.280
And I think just in the past year we've
seen a lot of great examples whether it's

00:41:32.280 --> 00:41:37.710
the Time's recent investigation
of nail salons which went viral.

00:41:37.710 --> 00:41:44.640
That was a long two-part series
where the popularity of Serial.

00:41:44.640 --> 00:41:48.940
I mean Serial was 12
one-hour installments.

00:41:48.940 --> 00:41:51.230
It was a true crime type of story.

00:41:51.230 --> 00:41:57.500
But, that debuted on
This American Life and

00:41:57.500 --> 00:42:02.810
by the second episode their audience had
ballooned to over a million listeners.

00:42:02.810 --> 00:42:07.460
It very much brought about
a renaissance in podcasting,

00:42:07.460 --> 00:42:10.060
which I think is very thrilling.

00:42:10.060 --> 00:42:14.980
But, that long story, even though
they chopped it up into chapters.

00:42:14.980 --> 00:42:19.642
I think there's like a human desire for
the long,

00:42:19.642 --> 00:42:25.540
engrossing, fabulously reported,
elegantly told story.

00:42:25.540 --> 00:42:30.531
And it takes us back to
the time of Charles Dickens,

00:42:30.531 --> 00:42:38.220
when kind of like Serial, Dickens'
novels were shipped out in chapters.

00:42:38.220 --> 00:42:45.200
And there would be Londoners standing on
the docks waiting for the latest chapters

00:42:45.200 --> 00:42:50.930
of The Old Curiosity Shop to be delivered
because they were on pins and needles.

00:42:50.930 --> 00:42:53.925
Is little Nell gonna be okay?

00:42:53.925 --> 00:42:59.100
And that's fiction, but I felt a real-life

00:42:59.100 --> 00:43:04.690
example of that was a story that I
championed at the Times where for

00:43:04.690 --> 00:43:11.080
an entire week we did a close-in series
on a homeless girl named Dasani,

00:43:11.080 --> 00:43:16.200
who lived in Fort Green, Brooklyn,
and against all the odds,

00:43:16.200 --> 00:43:24.140
was battling to be a great twelve-year-old
who was learning a lot in school.

00:43:24.140 --> 00:43:27.490
And it was readers were on
the edge of their seats

00:43:27.490 --> 00:43:32.200
because of the way those stories
were edited and reported.

00:43:32.200 --> 00:43:37.640
They were like page turners and
I just think that

00:43:37.640 --> 00:43:42.950
there's a demand for that journalism now,
even along with the short stuff.

00:43:42.950 --> 00:43:48.310
You may be surprised to here,
but BuzzFeed has a very

00:43:48.310 --> 00:43:53.830
large investigative reporting unit,
which does long series,

00:43:53.830 --> 00:43:59.060
some very good pieces and they have
15 full-time investigative reporters.

00:43:59.060 --> 00:44:04.300
That's almost as big an investigative
reporting unit as the New York Times has,

00:44:04.300 --> 00:44:08.560
so I'm very, very bullish on long form and

00:44:08.560 --> 00:44:14.130
I think what I would leave
all of you with is I think

00:44:14.130 --> 00:44:19.990
the ability to write a gripping,
clear narrative that that talent,

00:44:19.990 --> 00:44:26.200
because you need it in business too,
you need it in every field,

00:44:26.200 --> 00:44:29.560
it's never been a more
marketable skill than it is now.

00:44:29.560 --> 00:44:35.440
I think narrative hath
incredible power and

00:44:35.440 --> 00:44:37.990
that it's a skill that can be monetized.

00:44:37.990 --> 00:44:38.490
&gt;&gt; One more question?

00:44:42.660 --> 00:44:43.440
&gt;&gt; Hi Jill.

00:44:43.440 --> 00:44:45.527
&gt;&gt; Hi.
&gt;&gt; Janie Lorber, New York Times,

00:44:45.527 --> 00:44:48.195
Washington Bureau, 2008 through 2011.

00:44:48.195 --> 00:44:50.425
&gt;&gt; Wow, yeah.

00:44:50.425 --> 00:44:53.440
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] I'm really concerned
about the monetization because I

00:44:54.470 --> 00:44:57.760
went into journalism to be
an investigative reporter and

00:44:57.760 --> 00:45:00.950
came here because I was worried
those jobs wouldn't exist.

00:45:02.190 --> 00:45:04.700
How can you elaborate on how BuzzFeed is

00:45:04.700 --> 00:45:06.960
supporting such a large
investigative unit?

00:45:06.960 --> 00:45:10.200
Can you elaborate on whether we will
have public service journalism,

00:45:10.200 --> 00:45:13.240
investigative journalism in ten years?

00:45:13.240 --> 00:45:16.810
And how will we fund it?

00:45:16.810 --> 00:45:21.877
&gt;&gt; I said I'm bullish on it and
I really mean it, Janie,

00:45:21.877 --> 00:45:26.810
because I think obviously
investigative reporting

00:45:28.020 --> 00:45:32.060
isn't where BuzzFeed is
making most of its money.

00:45:32.060 --> 00:45:37.900
I mean it makes, I believe, most of
its money on branded advertising, and

00:45:37.900 --> 00:45:42.870
of course, on the humorous listicles

00:45:42.870 --> 00:45:47.682
that run every day that people
love to look at and share.

00:45:47.682 --> 00:45:53.000
But I think that many of

00:45:53.000 --> 00:45:59.820
these new places, almost as a prestige
thing, or to be taken seriously, or

00:45:59.820 --> 00:46:05.180
let's give them the credit that actually
do want to help perform a public good and

00:46:05.180 --> 00:46:08.970
inform the public, that as they mature and

00:46:08.970 --> 00:46:14.640
become real institutions, they want
to be doing quality journalism, too.

00:46:14.640 --> 00:46:20.030
And it isn't that different from
like back in the 1920s when

00:46:20.030 --> 00:46:24.585
the comics help support
the coverage of World War II.

00:46:24.585 --> 00:46:30.890
But it's gonna take some years more for

00:46:30.890 --> 00:46:38.546
us to know whether there is a stable
business model for journalism.

00:46:38.546 --> 00:46:42.950
And I guess where I've come out is,
you know.

00:46:42.950 --> 00:46:48.378
Marty Barron of the Washington Post was
out here recently in California and

00:46:48.378 --> 00:46:50.274
gave a fabulous speech and

00:46:50.274 --> 00:46:55.028
said, the important thing is to
stop mourning what we have lost.

00:46:55.028 --> 00:46:58.112
And I really believe that's important.

00:46:58.112 --> 00:47:03.374
But he and an academic named Clay Shirky,
who I'm very smart,

00:47:03.374 --> 00:47:09.670
kind of ascribed to it there isn't
going to be one business model answer.

00:47:09.670 --> 00:47:14.630
It's not like there's
some great mega solution

00:47:14.630 --> 00:47:19.120
sitting out there for
just someone really smart to discover.

00:47:19.120 --> 00:47:23.310
You have to try a lot of different things.

00:47:23.310 --> 00:47:27.610
So, I think for instance in New York some
people have said it doesn't make sense for

00:47:27.610 --> 00:47:32.120
the New York Times which is trying
to have a digital subscription

00:47:32.120 --> 00:47:36.090
model to suddenly be putting
its content on Facebook.

00:47:36.090 --> 00:47:38.850
But by being on Facebook
they are increasing

00:47:38.850 --> 00:47:41.850
the distribution of their story,
they're getting a bigger audience,

00:47:41.850 --> 00:47:44.850
and maybe they will get some
subscriptions that way.

00:47:44.850 --> 00:47:47.378
But I think you kind of have to do it all.

00:47:47.378 --> 00:47:50.710
And that somehow being an optimist,

00:47:50.710 --> 00:47:55.620
that's gonna bring quality
journalism to safe passage.

00:47:55.620 --> 00:48:00.528
I really believe that because
it's a societal necessity.

00:48:03.070 --> 00:48:04.670
&gt;&gt; One more question from Twitter?

00:48:04.670 --> 00:48:05.180
&gt;&gt; Sure, yeah.

00:48:05.180 --> 00:48:10.240
So you've been in an industry where you've
had to set your personal biases aside for

00:48:10.240 --> 00:48:11.420
a long time.

00:48:11.420 --> 00:48:12.983
And not the-
&gt;&gt; So, what are they?

00:48:12.983 --> 00:48:15.870
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Well, you knew it was coming.

00:48:15.870 --> 00:48:18.850
No, the question is now that
you're no longer part of

00:48:18.850 --> 00:48:22.500
a journalism organization and can speak
out on issues that you care about,

00:48:22.500 --> 00:48:24.490
whether it's ones more personal to you or

00:48:24.490 --> 00:48:28.520
ones you see going on in society, are you
gonna start doing that in your future?

00:48:28.520 --> 00:48:30.370
Are you planning on doing that, or?

00:48:30.370 --> 00:48:35.360
No I actually, the book I'm
working on is a reported book.

00:48:35.360 --> 00:48:40.610
It will be reported narrative and
I want to go out a reporter.

00:48:40.610 --> 00:48:46.655
I mean one of the really, if you can say
there was like a nice side to being fired.

00:48:46.655 --> 00:48:50.885
It was like almost immediately
I went to reporting and

00:48:50.885 --> 00:48:56.815
writing some pieces myself and
kind of rediscovering Jill Abramson

00:48:56.815 --> 00:49:01.895
the individual journalist, the lone wolf,
I guess, which I always was.

00:49:01.895 --> 00:49:07.403
And I want to hue to the journalism
standards that I always have.

00:49:07.403 --> 00:49:13.820
So you're not going to see me giving big
donations to the Clinton Foundation.

00:49:13.820 --> 00:49:16.592
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Thank you so much, Jill.

00:49:16.592 --> 00:49:19.370
I'm gonna have the honor to ask
you the last question of the day,

00:49:19.370 --> 00:49:22.840
which is a View From The Top
tradition now.

00:49:22.840 --> 00:49:24.480
What matters most to you and why?

00:49:26.330 --> 00:49:34.884
&gt;&gt; What matters most to me now is
having an opportunity to give back and

00:49:34.884 --> 00:49:42.370
to help younger people discover
a love of story telling and

00:49:42.370 --> 00:49:47.590
being able to write and report stories.

00:49:47.590 --> 00:49:53.860
So my teaching that I've
begun is very important.

00:49:53.860 --> 00:49:59.157
I'm very Interested because I do
think we live in an age of narrative.

00:49:59.157 --> 00:50:03.277
I'd love to work with
like a girls club and

00:50:03.277 --> 00:50:06.820
teach even younger girls to write.

00:50:06.820 --> 00:50:09.734
My family is vitally important to me,

00:50:09.734 --> 00:50:13.930
I'm gonna become a first-time
grandma pretty soon.

00:50:13.930 --> 00:50:14.895
&gt;&gt; Congratulations.

00:50:14.895 --> 00:50:19.459
I can't wait to start a new generation,

00:50:19.459 --> 00:50:23.761
with the love or reading and writing.

00:50:23.761 --> 00:50:24.486
So that's what matters to me.

00:50:24.486 --> 00:50:27.403
&gt;&gt; You can take your granddaughter to
go and hunt down the different houses.

00:50:27.403 --> 00:50:28.153
&gt;&gt; Yeah, right.

00:50:28.153 --> 00:50:31.843
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Well, with that, thank you so much,

00:50:31.843 --> 00:50:32.737
Jill Abramson.

00:50:32.737 --> 00:50:34.904
[APPLAUSE]

00:50:34.904 --> 00:50:39.800
[MUSIC]

